# Amazing Grace, strum and sing (repertoire plan: amazing-grace). John Newton's words (1779) on
# the anonymous tune New Britain (printed 1829), in G. Your part: a waltz strum, the bass note on
# beat 1 (string 6 fret 3 under G and G7, string 5 fret 3 under C, open string 4 under D7, open
# string 6 under Em), then down-up on beats 2 and 3. Chords: G (320003), C (x32010),
# D7 (xx0212), with G7 (320001) before C in the second half and Em (022000) under "blind but";
# both of those can be played as G until they are comfortable.
# The tune (second track) starts with a pickup on beat 3 and uses only G A B D E: in first
# position, low D (open string 4) to the high D (string 2, fret 3). The two quick notes on
# "-ing", "a", "was" and "but" (B then G) are sung on one syllable.
title: Amazing Grace
id: amazing-grace
composer: Words by John Newton; tune New Britain (anonymous American)
source: Words 1779; tune printed 1829, joined to these words in 1835; arranged for Sixstring Academy
arranger: Sixstring Academy
licence: Public domain; this arrangement is original to Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 3
tags: strumming, waltz, hymn, 3/4, pickup, lyrics
tempo: 84
time: 3/4
key: G
pickup: q

track: Your strum
role: rhythm
